(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-3-endocrine-quiz-version-3"]={"title":"Endocrine System — Anatomy Exam 3 (Set 3)","category":"Anatomy","questions":[{"q":"Which of the following is considered a PRIMARY endocrine organ rather than a tissue with a secondary endocrine role?","choices":["Adipose tissue","Heart","Pineal gland","Digestive tract"],"answer":2,"exp":"The pineal gland is a dedicated primary endocrine organ, unlike the heart, adipose tissue, and digestive tract, which are tissues with only secondary endocrine roles. — Slides 3–4 (Overview of the Endocrine System)"},{"q":"The pituitary gland is attached to the hypothalamus by which stalk?","choices":["Infundibulum","Isthmus","Median raphe","Pedicle"],"answer":0,"exp":"The pituitary (hypophysis) attaches to the hypothalamus via the infundibulum and sits in the hypophyseal fossa of the sella turcica. — Slide 10 (The Pituitary Gland)"},{"q":"The anterior lobe of the pituitary gland is also called the:","choices":["Pars nervosa","Neurohypophysis","Adenohypophysis","Median eminence"],"answer":2,"exp":"The adenohypophysis is the anterior lobe (glandular) of the pituitary. — Slide 10 (The Pituitary Gland)"},{"q":"The thyroid gland consists of two lobes connected by a bridge of tissue called the:","choices":["Isthmus","Infundibulum","Pyramidal lobe","Colloid"],"answer":0,"exp":"The thyroid is formed of two lobes connected by an isthmus, which lies opposite the 2nd and 3rd tracheal rings. — Slides 25, 29 (The Thyroid Gland)"},{"q":"How many parathyroid glands are usually present, and where are they located?","choices":["Two, within the sella turcica fossa","Four, on the posterior aspect of the thyroid","Six, embedded along the cervical trachea","Two, on the surface of the adrenal cortex"],"answer":1,"exp":"There are usually four parathyroid glands (one superior and one inferior on each side) on the posterior aspect of the thyroid. — Slides 43–44 (Parathyroid Glands)"},{"q":"Each suprarenal (adrenal) gland is subdivided into an outer cortex and an inner:","choices":["Capsule","Isthmus","Follicle","Medulla"],"answer":3,"exp":"Each adrenal gland has an outer cortex (three zones) and an inner medulla (chromaffin cells). — Slide 82 (The Suprarenal Glands)"},{"q":"The endocrine portion of the pancreas is organized into clusters of cells called the:","choices":["Pancreatic acinar exocrine units","Pancreatic islets (of Langerhans)","Thyroid-type colloid follicles","Adrenal cortical cell cords"],"answer":1,"exp":"The pancreatic islets (islets of Langerhans) are the endocrine cells; the acini are the exocrine cells. — Slide 52 (Anatomy of the Pancreas)"},{"q":"The hypothalamus releases which two hormones to the posterior pituitary?","choices":["Insulin and glucagon from islets","Thyrotropin and corticotropin","Antidiuretic hormone and oxytocin","Cortisol and aldosterone output"],"answer":2,"exp":"The hypothalamus produces ADH and oxytocin, which are stored/released by the neurohypophysis (posterior pituitary). — Slide 9 (Hypothalamus and Endocrine Regulation)"},{"q":"The structural unit of the thyroid gland is the thyroid follicle, whose wall is composed of which epithelium?","choices":["Pseudostratified columnar epithelium","Stratified squamous epithelium","Simple cuboidal epithelium","Transitional epithelium"],"answer":2,"exp":"Each thyroid follicle consists of simple cuboidal follicular epithelium surrounding a central mass of colloid. — Slide 30 (Histology of thyroid gland)"},{"q":"The parafollicular (C) cells of the thyroid secrete which hormone?","choices":["Cortisol","Thyroxine (T4)","Parathyroid hormone","Calcitonin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Parafollicular (C) cells sit near the follicular basement lamina and secrete calcitonin; they are larger and lighter-staining than follicular cells. — Slide 31 (Parafollicular / C cells)"},{"q":"Within the parathyroid gland, which cells produce parathyroid hormone?","choices":["Principal (chief) cells","Oxyphil (eosinophilic) cells","Follicular (colloid) cells","Adrenal chromaffin cells"],"answer":0,"exp":"Principal (chief) cells, with slightly eosinophilic cytoplasm, produce PTH; oxyphil cells are larger and their function is uncertain. — Slide 46 (Histology of parathyroid glands)"},{"q":"In the pars distalis, the acidophils (reddish cytoplasm) secrete which hormones?","choices":["Thyrotropin and corticotropin","Growth hormone and prolactin","Gonadotropins FSH and LH","Vasopressin and oxytocin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Acidophils (~10% of pars distalis cells) secrete growth hormone and prolactin; basophils secrete TSH, ACTH, FSH, and LH. — Slide 21 (Pars distalis)"},{"q":"The zona glomerulosa of the adrenal cortex secretes which class of hormones?","choices":["Sex steroids (androgens)","Glucocorticoids (mainly cortisol)","Mineralocorticoids (aldosterone)","Catecholamines (epinephrine)"],"answer":2,"exp":"The zona glomerulosa secretes mineralocorticoids (aldosterone); fasciculata secretes glucocorticoids; reticularis secretes androgens. — Slide 87 (Histology of Adrenal Glands)"},{"q":"The principal cells of the adrenal medulla, which secrete epinephrine and norepinephrine, are the:","choices":["Podocytes","Chief cells","Follicular cells","Chromaffin cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"Chromaffin cells are modified postganglionic sympathetic neurons (lacking dendrites/axons) that secrete adrenaline and noradrenaline. — Slide 89 (Adrenal Medulla)"},{"q":"The pars nervosa of the posterior pituitary contains supporting glial-like cells called:","choices":["Pituicytes","Chromophobes","Acidophils","Oxyphils"],"answer":0,"exp":"The pars nervosa contains pituicytes plus bundles of nerve fibers of the hypothalamo-hypophyseal tract and Herring bodies. — Slide 22 (Pars nervosa)"},{"q":"Active thyroid follicles generally exhibit which change in their follicular epithelium?","choices":["Conversion to stratified epithelium","Flattened squamous epithelium","Loss of the basement membrane","Taller ('high') epithelium"],"answer":3,"exp":"Active follicles generally have 'high' (taller) follicular epithelium, reflecting increased secretory activity. — Slide 30 (Histology of thyroid gland)"},{"q":"The pituitary gland sits in the hypophyseal fossa of which bone?","choices":["Temporal bone","Ethmoid bone","Occipital bone","Sphenoid bone"],"answer":3,"exp":"The pituitary lies in the hypophyseal fossa of the sella turcica of the sphenoid bone, covered by the diaphragma sellae. — Slide 13 (Pituitary Gland)"},{"q":"The superior thyroid artery, which supplies the thyroid gland, is a branch of which vessel?","choices":["Thyrocervical trunk","External carotid artery","Arch of the aorta","Internal carotid artery"],"answer":1,"exp":"The superior thyroid artery arises from the external carotid artery; the inferior thyroid artery arises from the thyrocervical trunk. — Slide 32 (Blood supply of thyroid gland)"},{"q":"The recurrent laryngeal nerve is closely related to which artery at the posterior surface of the thyroid gland?","choices":["Inferior thyroid artery","Superior thyroid artery","Thyroidea ima artery","Facial artery"],"answer":0,"exp":"The inferior thyroid artery becomes related to the recurrent laryngeal nerve at the posterior surface of the thyroid — a key surgical relationship. — Slide 32 (Blood supply of thyroid gland)"},{"q":"The tail of the pancreas extends toward which organ?","choices":["The gallbladder","The liver","The spleen","The stomach fundus"],"answer":2,"exp":"The rounded head connects to the duodenum and the pointed tail extends toward the hilum of the spleen. — Slides 50, 56 (The Pancreas)"},{"q":"The suprarenal (adrenal) glands are located attached to which border of the kidneys?","choices":["The inferior border","The superior border","The lateral border","The hilum"],"answer":1,"exp":"The adrenal glands sit on the superior border of the kidneys, enclosed in renal fascia but separated by fibrous tissue. — Slides 81, 86 (The Suprarenal Glands)"},{"q":"The right suprarenal vein drains into the IVC, whereas the left suprarenal vein drains into the:","choices":["Hepatic portal vein","Left renal vein","Right azygos vein","Left splenic vein"],"answer":1,"exp":"Venous drainage is asymmetric: the right suprarenal vein → IVC; the left suprarenal vein → left renal vein. — Slide 86 (The Suprarenal Glands)"},{"q":"The thyroid gland moves up and down with swallowing because it is enclosed within which fascia, attached to the larynx?","choices":["Renal fascia","Carotid sheath","Prevertebral fascia","Pretracheal fascia"],"answer":3,"exp":"The thyroid lies within the pretracheal fascia, connected above to the thyroid cartilage, so it elevates during deglutition. — Slide 28 (Thyroid relationships)"},{"q":"The adrenal medulla contains which prominent vascular structure?","choices":["A large central vein","A fenestrated glomerulus","A portal venous plexus","A lymphatic sinus"],"answer":0,"exp":"The adrenal medulla contains a large central vein into which medullary blood drains. — Slide 89 (Adrenal Medulla)"},{"q":"Parathyroid hormone (PTH), which raises blood calcium, is produced by which gland?","choices":["Adrenal cortex","Thyroid follicular cells","Parathyroid glands","Pineal gland"],"answer":2,"exp":"PTH is produced by the parathyroid chief cells and raises blood calcium, opposing calcitonin. — Slides 44, 46 (Parathyroid Glands)"},{"q":"Calcitonin, which tends to lower blood calcium, is secreted by which thyroid cells?","choices":["Parafollicular (C) cells","Follicular (colloid) cells","Parathyroid chief cells","Parathyroid oxyphil cells"],"answer":0,"exp":"Calcitonin is secreted by the thyroid's parafollicular (C) cells and acts to lower blood calcium, opposing PTH. — Slide 31 (Parafollicular / C cells)"},{"q":"Calcitriol, the active form of vitamin D involved in calcium regulation, is produced by which organ?","choices":["The thyroid gland","The kidney","The parathyroid glands","The adrenal medulla"],"answer":1,"exp":"The kidney produces calcitriol (and erythropoietin); calcitriol increases intestinal calcium absorption. — Slide 5 (Figure 19.1 — Kidney)"},{"q":"The three hormones most directly involved in calcium metabolism are:","choices":["Aldosterone, ADH, and ANP","Insulin, glucagon, and cortisol","T3, T4, and TSH","PTH, calcitonin, and calcitriol"],"answer":3,"exp":"Calcium balance is governed chiefly by PTH (raises Ca2+), calcitonin (lowers Ca2+), and calcitriol (active vitamin D). — Slides 5, 31, 44 (calcium-regulating hormones)"},{"q":"The oxyphil cells of the parathyroid gland are notable because they:","choices":["Are modified postganglionic sympathetic neurons","Secrete calcitonin to lower blood calcium levels","Form the colloid-filled follicles of the thyroid","Are larger and very eosinophilic, possibly also secreting PTH"],"answer":3,"exp":"Oxyphil cells are larger and very eosinophilic; their function is uncertain but they may also secrete PTH. — Slide 46 (Histology of parathyroid glands)"},{"q":"The oxyphil cells of the parathyroid gland are larger and very eosinophilic; their function is:","choices":["To store thyroid colloid precursor","To secrete calcitonin and lower calcium","Unknown, though they may also secrete PTH","To produce epinephrine and norepinephrine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Oxyphil cells are larger and very eosinophilic; their function is unknown but they may also secrete PTH. — Slide 46 (Histology of parathyroid glands)"}]};
